# Docker Compose Management Guide

This guide defines the rules for modifying the [docker-compose.yml](mdc:docker-compose.yml) file in this project. Follow these guidelines whenever you need to add, update, or remove services, networks, volumes, or other Docker Compose configurations.

## General Guidelines

- Use 2-space indentation for all entries
- Use kebab-case for service, network, and volume names
- Group related services together
- List services in order of dependency (base services first)
- Always include descriptive healthchecks for services
- Define explicit dependencies using depends_on
- Use environment variables or secrets for sensitive information
- Apply the Principle of Least Privilege (PoLP) when superuser permissions are not strictly required
- Document non-obvious configurations with comments
- Specify exact versions for all services (e.g., postgres:17-alpine not postgres:latest)
- Document version changes in [CHANGELOG.md](mdc:CHANGELOG.md)

## Service Structure

### Required Fields

Each service must include:

- image or build specification
- network attachment
- appropriate healthcheck

### Volume Mounts
- Use relative paths for local directories
- Mount application code to /var/www/html
- Use named volumes for persistent data

### Port Mapping
- Format as HOST:CONTAINER
- Avoid conflicts with standard ports (80, 443, etc.)
- Document port purpose in comments if not obvious

## Service-Specific Guidelines

### PHP-FPM Service

The php-fpm service is built from a custom Dockerfile:

```yaml
php-fpm:
  image: phpish:dev
  build:
    context: ./docker
    args:
      PHP_VERSION: 8.3
      COMPOSER_VERSION: 2.8.9
      XDEBUG_VERSION: 3.3.2
      REDIS_VERSION: 6.1.0
```

#### Configuration
- When updating PHP version, ensure compatibility with extensions
- When updating extensions, check compatibility with PHP version
- Document all version changes in CHANGELOG.md
- Always mount ./web:/var/www/html for application code
- Always include dependent services

### Nginx Service

```yaml
nginx:
  image: nginx:1.28-alpine
  volumes:
    - ./web:/var/www/html
    - ./docker/nginx/nginx.conf:/etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf
```

#### Configuration
- Always mount the nginx.conf file to /etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf
- Mount the same application directory as php-fpm
- Map port 80 to host port 80 by default
- Always depend on php-fpm service

### Node Service

```yaml
node:
  image: node:22-alpine
  working_dir: /var/www/html
  user: node
  volumes:
    - ./web:/var/www/html
```

#### Configuration
- Set working_dir to /var/www/html
- Use node user for security
- Use sleep infinity command for long-running container

### Postgres Service

```yaml
postgres:
  image: postgres:17-alpine
  volumes:
    - postgres-data:/var/lib/postgresql/data
  environment:
    POSTGRES_DB_FILE: /run/secrets/DB_DATABASE
    POSTGRES_USER_FILE: /run/secrets/DB_USERNAME
    POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE: /run/secrets/DB_PASSWORD
```

#### Configuration
- Use named volume for data persistence
- Use _FILE environment variables with secrets
- Define corresponding secrets in the secrets section

### Redis Service

```yaml
redis:
  image: redis:8-alpine
  volumes:
    - redis-data:/data
```

#### Configuration
- Use named volume for data persistence

## Networks and Volumes

### Networks

```yaml
networks:
  mainnet:
```

- Use mainnet as the primary network
- Create additional networks only when isolation is required

### Volumes

```yaml
volumes:
  postgres-data:
  redis-data:
```

- Use service-name-data naming convention
- Define all persistent volumes in the volumes section
- Do not use external volumes unless necessary

## Secrets Management

```yaml
secrets:
  DB_DATABASE:
    environment: DB_DATABASE
  DB_USERNAME:
    environment: DB_USERNAME
  DB_PASSWORD:
    environment: DB_PASSWORD
```

- Use environment variables as the source for secrets
- Reference secrets in services using _FILE suffix for environment variables
- Mount secrets to appropriate paths in the container

## Examples

### Example 1: Adding a New Service

To add a new service (e.g., Mailhog for email testing):

```yaml
mailhog:
  image: mailhog/mailhog:v1.0.1
  ports:
    - 1025:1025
    - 8025:8025
  networks:
    - mainnet
  healthcheck:
    test: ["CMD", "nc", "-z", "localhost", "8025"]
    interval: 30s
    timeout: 10s
    retries: 5
    start_period: 10s
```

### Example 2: Updating a Service Version

To update PHP version from 8.3 to 8.4:

```yaml
php-fpm:
  image: phpish:dev
  build:
    context: ./docker
    args:
      PHP_VERSION: 8.4  # Updated from 8.3
      COMPOSER_VERSION: 2.8.9
      XDEBUG_VERSION: 3.3.2
      REDIS_VERSION: 6.1.0
```

Remember to:

1. Test compatibility with all extensions
2. Update CHANGELOG.md with the version change

### Example 3: Adding Environment Variables

To add environment variables to a service:

```yaml
php-fpm:
  # ... existing configuration ...
  environment:
    APP_ENV: development
    APP_DEBUG: "true"
    REDIS_HOST: redis
```

### Example 4: Adding a Volume

To add a new volume for a service:

```yaml
services:
  new-service:
    # ... service configuration ...
    volumes:
      - new-service-data:/path/in/container

volumes:
  # ... existing volumes ...
  new-service-data:
```

